---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `I have a list of prize numbers. Let someone type a position
and tell them what is there. People type nonsense all the
time so it should not fall over.`;

const SampleOutput = `Prizes: 4 available.
Which position? 7

There is no prize at position 7.
Try a number from 1 to 4.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-4" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 4
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Safe Lookup</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code
            code={Brief}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="What they asked for"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-4">Set 4</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Start with the array already filled in.</li>
            <li>A position outside the list must produce a sensible message, not <code>undefined</code>.</li>
            <li>Decide whether positions start at 1 or 0 for the person using it, and write your decision down. Both are defensible; only one matches how people count.</li>
            <li><b>The same out-of-range lookup in exam notation would end the program with an error.</b> JavaScript just hands you <code>undefined</code>. Note that difference in your requirements list.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">Test 0, 1, the last valid position, and one past it. All four should behave sensibly.</p>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Optional, and it will save you time here.</b> Those four tests are
            four separate runs. Wrap the lookup in a loop that repeats while{" "}
            <code>confirm("Look up another?")</code> says yes and you can do all
            four in one run, which is also how a real lookup tool would work. This
            is a single loop, not a loop inside a loop.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-safe-lookup</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
